  "summary": "Recent preprints and papers report method-dependent accuracy for variant, repeat and assembly tools, motivating dataset-specific benchmarking.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational methods for genomic variation and structure judged against explicit benchmarks or ground truth. Preprints report dataset-dependent performance: five imputation programs recover 40-76% of 52 experimentally genotyped human inversions, with Minimac4 and IMPUTE5 performing best, and noncoding genetic-constraint metrics vary unevenly across the genome, with Gnocchi declining as GC content rises. Methods work also covers RFhap, which phases long reads by parental k-mer markers before assembly, and TandemTwister, which reports 99.4% recall on PacBio HiFi data. A published Holstein phased pangenome graph from 40 haploid assemblies improves structural variant genotyping and GWAS-based trait mapping, while TEvarSim supplies VCF ground truth for transposable element benchmarking. Performance remains specific to dataset and method.",
